course: dictionary
lesson: 10449
title: Using context: truncheon
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A truncheon is a short, thick stick. Police officers carry it as a weapon. You may not know this word, but the sentences around it give you clues. In «a pair of pistols, a policeman's truncheon, several sets of handcuffs», the items are things police use, so a truncheon is probably something police carry too.
explain: Look at what the police are doing in «policemen in their helmets, waving truncheons». They are waving sticks and guns, which tells you the truncheon is used in a fight or to stop people. A good check is to swap in your meaning: «policemen waving short thick sticks» still makes sense.
explain: The word can also be linked to a time and place. In history lessons, Victorian police, called Bobbies or Peelers, carried wooden truncheons. Today police carry batons. If you read about police in the United States, you might find the word nightstick instead.
fact: A truncheon is a short, thick stick carried by police officers as a weapon.
fact: In the US, a truncheon is called a nightstick or a baton.
fact: Victorian police, known as Bobbies or Peelers, carried wooden truncheons, along with rattles or whistles.
fact: Today police carry batons.
fact: The word truncheon also appears in Punch and Judy shows.
exercise: In «a policeman's truncheon», what kind of thing is a truncheon? | A long pole with an axe-blade | A bowler hat worn in the city | *A short stick that police carry | A flask for drinks
exercise: In «policemen in their helmets, waving truncheons», what are the policemen holding? | Long poles with axe-blades | Rattles to make a noise | Bowler hats | *Short sticks, used as weapons
exercise: What did Victorian police carry besides wooden truncheons? | Halberds | Sandwich-cases | *Rattles or whistles | Pot-hats
test: In the US, what is a truncheon called? | A cutlass | A flask | A rattle | *A nightstick
test: What is a halberd? | A pistol | A short, thick stick | A bowler hat | *A long pole with an axe-blade
exam: Victorian police carried wooden truncheons, but what do police carry today? | Flasks | Halberds | Rattles only | *Batons
